{
 "best_candidate": 14,
 "instruction": "Reply with sarcasm as a quiet, personal reaction: in a calm first-person voice, pretend you are glad about, or were hoping for, the exact bad or dull part of what the person said. The reader must be able to tell you mean the opposite.\n\nStep 1 (silently): find the downside. If the person complains, use that thing. If they sound proud, neutral, or happy (a new recipe, a book club change), mock the cost or likely bad result instead: the burnt pasta, skipping the novel, the long workout. Never just agree with a plan. That reads as sincere and fails.\n\nStep 2: write ONE sentence, 8\u201314 words, never more than 16.\n- Use \"I am so glad\" or \"I was so hoping\" and name the specific detail from their message.\n- Include one plain word that shows it is a waste or a downside: \"whole,\" \"just,\" \"instead of,\" \"again,\" \"all night,\" \"skip.\"\n- Speak about yourself or the person (\"I,\" \"you,\" \"we\"), not about how the world works.\n- Never use: \"clearly,\" \"obviously,\" \"apparently,\" \"of course,\" \"standard,\" \"designed,\" \"maximum,\" \"efficient,\" \"optional,\" \"famously,\" \"oh,\" \"wow,\" \"great,\" \"love,\" \"perfect,\" \"nothing says,\" \"I'm sure,\" or exclamation marks.\n- Use plain, soft words, like a sincere thank-you note. No vivid imagery, no exaggeration.\n\nExamples:\n- \"I am so glad we spent the whole meeting just scheduling.\" (pointless meeting)\n- \"I was so hoping we would skip the novel for the school board.\" (book club)\n- \"I am so glad the whole office sang to you again.\" (awful singing)\n- \"I was so hoping for smoke alarms with my pasta tonight.\" (new recipe)",
